  "answer_text": "The iconic scene where Del Boy falls through the bar is from the episode **\"Yuppy Love\"**! Here are all the details:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcfa The Episode\n**\"Yuppy Love\"** was the first episode of Series 6 of *Only Fools and Horses*, airing on **8th January 1989** on BBC1, with a viewing figure of **13.9 million**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf78 The Scene\nIn a wine bar, Del is leaning against a folding section at the end of the bar when he spots an attractive young woman looking at him. A barman comes along and lifts up the section Del was leaning against to get through, but doesn't put it back down, leaving a gap \u2014 all behind Del's back without him noticing. Del then says *\"Play it nice and cool, son. Nice and cool, you know what I mean...\"* and leans back, falling straight through the gap onto the floor.\n\nJust as funny was Trigger's baffled reaction to Del's sudden disappearance.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfad Behind the Scenes\n- The scene was inspired by writer **John Sullivan** watching a man do the exact same thing in a real wine bar. Sullivan thought it funny for the man's body language trying to recover his cool.\n\n- Sullivan originally wanted a slip, stumble, and tree-like fall, but **David Jason** thought Del should go all the way over \u2014 starting to go sideways without looking in the direction of the fall, which Jason felt was the key to the scene. There was a hidden crash mat, but it was a hard shot to get as it was difficult not to look where he was falling.\n\n- A rehearsal script that went up for auction revealed that **Trigger wasn't supposed to be in the scene originally**. However, Roger Lloyd Pack (who played Trigger) happened to be there on the day, and John Sullivan decided he should be included after all.\n\n- Roger Lloyd Pack later revealed that David Jason **never did the fall in rehearsal**, so it was a genuine surprise when it happened during filming \u2014 making his stunned reaction all the more authentic.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfc6 Legacy\nDel Boy's fall through the bar has become **one of the most celebrated sitcom scenes ever**. David Jason says he gets people asking him about that fall all the time, but he's happy to be remembered for something so iconic.",
